{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The target answers the cross-city variation half of the question (sky view factor scaling with building height/density) but misattributes the primary cause of the baseline nocturnal UHI gap itself. It frames the warmer city as mainly a 'radiative-geometry effect' of reduced sky view trapping longwave radiation. But the dominant nocturnal UHI mechanism is the slow release of stored daytime heat by high thermal-mass urban materials; street-canyon geometry and reduced sky view is a secondary or modifying factor. By demoting thermal mass to a 'secondary role' and making reduced radiative cooling the primary explanation, the answer gives a less accurate single-mechanism account of why the city is several degrees warmer at night. The cross-city modulator it identifies (building height/density) actually affects nocturnal UHI largely because taller, denser canyons also enclose more thermally massive fabric and reduce ventilation, not because longwave trapping alone dominates.","premise":"The question asks why the city stays several degrees warmer at night; the strongest single mechanism is thermal storage/release from urban materials, not longwave trapping by street-canyon geometry.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"d6f1c3f1bd2d","quote":"The nocturnal UHI gap is primarily a radiative-geometry effect: dense urban street canyons have a lower sky view factor, which traps longwave radiation and suppresses radiative cooling after sunset"},{"block":"e2bb6b1cf3a4","quote":"with thermal mass playing a secondary role."}],"counterexample":null}]}